# overview
Follow this exact ClickOps runbook on AWS Console for your current dev model:

Goal:

Use one existing default subnet as public
Create one new private subnet
Deploy dashboard-service on public EC2 with Elastic IP
Deploy counting-service on private EC2
SSH to dashboard first, then hop to counting
Start both services and validate traffic
Phase 1: Identify VPC and existing subnet

Open AWS Console.
Go to VPC.
Click Your VPCs.
Find the default VPC in your target region.
Note its VPC ID.
Click Subnets.
Filter by that VPC ID.
Choose one existing subnet to be your Public Subnet.
Phase 2: Confirm public subnet settings

In Subnets, select the chosen public subnet.
Click Actions, then Edit subnet settings.
Enable Auto-assign public IPv4 address.
Save.
Go to Route tables.
Find the route table associated with that subnet.
Open Routes tab.
Confirm there is a default route:
Destination 0.0.0.0/0, Target igw-xxxxxxxx.
If missing, add route to the Internet Gateway.
Phase 3: Create private subnet

Go to VPC, then Subnets.
Click Create subnet.
VPC: select your default VPC.
Subnet name: private-app-subnet.
AZ: pick same AZ as public subnet for simple dev.
IPv4 CIDR: choose a non-overlapping block, for example 172.31.100.0/24.
Click Create subnet.
Select this new private subnet.
Click Actions, Edit subnet settings.
Disable Auto-assign public IPv4 address.
Save.
Phase 4: Create private route table

Go to VPC, Route tables.
Click Create route table.
Name: private-rt.
VPC: your default VPC.
Click Create.
Open Subnet associations tab.
Click Edit subnet associations.
Select private-app-subnet.
Save associations.
Open Routes tab.
Keep only local route.
Do not add 0.0.0.0/0 route for now.
Phase 5: Create security groups

Go to EC2, Security Groups.

Click Create security group.

Name: dashboard-sg.

VPC: default VPC.

Inbound rules:
Type HTTP, Port 80, Source Anywhere IPv4.
Type HTTPS, Port 443, Source Anywhere IPv4, only if using HTTPS now.
Type SSH, Port 22, Source My IP or your fixed trusted CIDR.

Outbound: keep default Allow all.

Create security group.

Click Create security group again.

Name: counting-sg.

VPC: default VPC.

Inbound rules:
Custom TCP, Port 9001, Source dashboard-sg.
SSH, Port 22, Source dashboard-sg.

Outbound: keep default Allow all.

Create security group.

Phase 6: Create key pair

Go to EC2, Key pairs.
Click Create key pair.
Name: dev-key.
Type: RSA.
Format: pem.
Create and download file.
Phase 7: Launch dashboard EC2 in public subnet

Go to EC2, Instances, Launch instances.
Name: dashboard-ec2.
Choose AMI, for example Amazon Linux 2023.
Instance type: t3.micro.
Key pair: dev-key.
Network settings:
VPC: default VPC.
Subnet: chosen public subnet.
Auto-assign public IP: enable.
Firewall: select existing security group dashboard-sg.
Storage defaults are fine for dev.
Click Launch instance.
Phase 8: Launch counting EC2 in private subnet

Launch another instance.
Name: counting-ec2.
Same AMI and type.
Key pair: dev-key.
Network settings:
VPC: default VPC.
Subnet: private-app-subnet.
Auto-assign public IP: disable.
Firewall: select existing security group counting-sg.
Launch.
Phase 9: Allocate and attach Elastic IP to dashboard

Go to EC2, Elastic IPs.
Click Allocate Elastic IP address.
Click Allocate.
Select the new Elastic IP.
Click Actions, Associate Elastic IP address.
Resource type: Instance.
Instance: dashboard-ec2.
Private IP: primary.
Associate.
Use this Elastic IP for browser and SSH.
Phase 10: SSH and hop to private instance

SSH from your laptop to dashboard using Elastic IP and pem key.
Get counting private IP from EC2 console.
From dashboard host, SSH to counting private IP using key forwarding or copied key as your method.
Phase 11: Deploy and run services with systemd

On dashboard host, install runtime dependencies for dashboard-service.
Deploy dashboard-service files.
Configure dashboard-service to listen on desired port.
On counting host, install runtime dependencies for counting-service.
Deploy counting-service files.
Configure counting-service to listen on port 9001.
Configure dashboard-service to call counting private IP on port 9001.
Create systemd service for dashboard-service.
Create systemd service for counting-service.
Enable and start both services.
Check status and logs on each host.
Phase 12: Validate success

From browser, open dashboard Elastic IP and confirm service response.
From dashboard host, call counting private IP on port 9001 and confirm response.
From your laptop, confirm counting private IP is not reachable.
Confirm SSH to dashboard works only from trusted source.
Confirm SSH to counting works only from dashboard host.
Common issues if it fails

Wrong subnet association to route table.
Dashboard SG missing SSH from your trusted IP.
Counting SG missing rule from dashboard-sg on port 9001.
Counting instance accidentally launched with public IP.
Service not bound to correct interface and port.
Systemd service not enabled or failed to start.
